    "title": "Hypothetical Local LLM Inference: 18 vs 24 Tokens/Sec - What's Missing?",
    "body": "I've encountered some hypothetical performance figures for local LLM inference on a single machine, showing 18 tokens/sec and 24 tokens/sec for two different runtimes. While these numbers provide a basic comparison point, the brief explicitly states this is a hypothetical experiment and not measured results. Crucially, factors like time to first token and the actual quality of the generated answers were not part of this hypothetical test. This means we can't conclude which runtime is universally better, as speed alone doesn't tell the whole story. The provided figures (F1, F2) are limited to this specific setup and prompt.\n\nThis raises questions about how to interpret such synthetic benchmarks. What other metrics are critical for evaluating local inference performance beyond raw token generation speed, especially when factors like latency and output relevance are unknown? F3 and F4 highlight these limitations.",
    "fact_refs": ["F1", "F2", "F3", "F4"],
    "plan": "Discuss the limitations of the hypothetical benchmark, focusing on what metrics are missing beyond raw token speed. Invite discussion on the importance of time-to-first-token and answer quality in practical local LLM use cases, given the hypothetical nature of the provided data."
